00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.400
Moving right along to section two, pure
versus speculative

254
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:07.800
risk.

255
00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.160
To an insurer, not all risks are created

256
00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.300
equal. In fact, there are two very
distinct types of

257
00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.980
risk we need to understand. First up is
speculative

258
00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:18.640
risk. This is a situation where

259
00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:21.220
there's a possibility of either a
financial loss or a financial

260
00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.140
gain. Think of walking into a casino

261
00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:27.100
and putting chips on a roulette table or
sinking your life savings into a brand new

262
00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:27.640
tech startup.

263
00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.140
You could strike it rich and double your
money or you could lose the entire

264
00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.300
investment. There's

265
00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:35.200
an upside and a downside. The second type
is pure

266
00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.080
risk. This is the chance of financial loss

267
00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.000
with absolutely zero chance of financial

268
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.740
gain. If a severe storm tears the roof off
your house,

269
00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.540
there's no upside there. You literally
cannot profit from that

270
00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:45.700
event. You can only lose.

271
00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.480
Now, the societal implication of this
distinction is incredibly

272
00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:50.360
fascinating.

273
00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.060
Insurance companies will only protect pure

274
00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.480
risk. You simply cannot buy an insurance
policy

275
00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.120
to protect against a speculative

276
00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.080
failure. Just imagine if you could buy a
policy that paid out

277
00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:03.540
a million dollars if your blackjackhand
busted or if your new restaurant failed to

278
00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:04.040
attack customers.

279
00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:08.640
What would that do to society? It would
instantly incentivize reckless behavior
and

280
00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:09.260
constant failure.

281
00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:13.280
It's just not in the interest of society
to allow people to profit from their own

282
00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:17.580
gambles. When there is absolutely no
chance to profit from a loss, when the
risk is

283
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:17.880
pure

284
00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:22.560
unadulterated tragedy or accident, that is
precisely where society steps in to offer

285
00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:22.920
support

286
00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:27.740
and stabilization through insurance. Which
brings us to section three, risks

287
00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:28.520
versus hazards.

288
00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.520
So we've got a really solid grasp on what
a risk is, but in the professional

289
00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:33.380
insurance world,

290
00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:36.320
you'll hear the word hazard constantly and
it means something very

291
00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:36.740
specific.

292
00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.460
A risk, as we established, is the actual
chance of a loss

293
00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.560
happening. A hazard, on the other hand,

294
00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.120
is any condition that increases that risk
or increases the sheer amount of damage if

295
00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:47.160
a

296
00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:51.380
loss actually does occur. To make this
super concrete, think of a risk as a tiny

297
00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:52.040
floating spark.

298
00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:55.520
A hazard is a giant puddle of gasoline
sitting on the floor right beneath

299
00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:55.680
it.

300
00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.080
The puddle isn't the fire itself, but its
presence makes the chance of a

301
00:11:59.080 --> 00:11:59.820
catastrophic fire

302
00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.780
significantly more likely and much more

303
00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.340
severe. Hazards can be broken down even
further,

304
00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.080
revealing a lot about how underwriters
evaluate the

305
00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.000
world. First, there are physical

306
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:09.440
hazards.

307
00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:11.320
These are tangible observable

308
00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:15.340
conditions. If a business operates a
machine shop in an old wooden

309
00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:19.280
building and they do heavy welding inside
every day, well, the building's age, its

310
00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:19.800
combustible

311
00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.760
construction, and the sparks from the
welding are all physical

312
00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.120
hazards. But then, we dive into

313
00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.140
the fascinating psychology of moral

314
00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:31.120
hazards. These are subjective
characteristics of the insured

315
00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:34.600
person themselves that actually increase
the likelihood of a

316
00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:34.840
loss.

317
00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:39.240
For instance, consider someone who
subconsciously stops locking their front
door

318
00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.060
every time they run

319
00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:43.640
out to the store simply because they know
they've recently bought top-tier theft

320
00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:44.040
insurance.

321
00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.580
Their relaxed attitude and reduced
caution? That is a moral

322
00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.720
hazard. An insurer really has to

323
00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.240
evaluate both the physical state of the
property and the character and habits of
the

324
00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:53.800
person holding

325
00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:58.540
the policy. Let's transition over to
section four, four ways to handle

326
00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.560
risk. Now we get to the

327
00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.300
highly practical part of the textbook, the
rule of

328
00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:06.060
four. It neatly lays out the only four
methods

329
00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:10.340
we realistically have for managing risk,
eliminating, controlling, retaining, and

330
00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:10.720
transferring.

331
00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:14.720
And think about your own life for a
second. Whether you realize that or not,
you

332
00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:15.360
actually use these

333
00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.120
four tools every single day to navigate
the

334
00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:19.500
world. Let's walk through exactly what
each of these

335
00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:20.980
looks like when applied to major financial

336
00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.100
decisions. The very first method is
eliminating

337
00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:29.220
risk entirely. Imagine a couple, let's
call them Chris and Jin, who just

338
00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.160
bought a charming

339
00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:31.540
old house on the edge of

340
00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:35.560
town. It comes with a massive, terrifying,
rusty wood stove right in

341
00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.800
the basement, and they have absolutely no
idea how to safely operate

342
00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:40.220
it. Because they're deeply

343
00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:44.060
worried it might cause a devastating fire,
they hire a contractor to just tear it

344
00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:44.400
out and

345
00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:48.260
permanently remove it from the home. By
taking that definitive action, they've

346
00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:49.280
completely eliminated

347
00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:53.360
the risk of a fire starting from that
specific wood stove, boom, problem

348
00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:54.960
solved. However, elimination

349
00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.100
isn't always practical or even

350
00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:00.480
possible. I mean, you could eliminate the
risk of a car accident by

351
00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.840
selling your car and literally never
leaving your

352
00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:05.020
house. But if you need that car to commute
to

353
00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.800
work and feed your family, you're going to
have to find another way to handle the

354
00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:08.060
risk.

355
00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:12.240
When we can't eliminate a danger, we move
to the second method, controlling

356
00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:13.600
risk. This is all

357
00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:17.160
about the psychology of mitigation. It's
an admission that, okay, we can't be

358
00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:17.800
perfectly safe,

359
00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:19.480
but we can definitely be significantly

360
00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.100
smarter. Controlling risk means taking
active steps to

361
00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.160
reduce the frequency or the severity of a

362
00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.700
loss. Taking an advanced defensive driver
training

363
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.360
course makes you a sharper, better

364
00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.100
driver. Installing a state-of-the-art
monitored fire alarm

365
00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.320
system helps you catch a fire in its
absolute earliest

366
00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.240
seconds. But we do have to be realistic

367
00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.620
here. Controlling a risk is never a total

368
00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:41.280
solution. A driving course doesn't
guarantee you'll never

369
00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:44.860
be broadsided at an intersection, and a
fire alarm doesn't magically prevent wires

370
00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:45.360
from sparking.

371
00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.140
It simply helps control and minimize the
resulting

372
00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:48.540
damage.

373
00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:53.620
The third method is retaining risk, and
this is where our financial strategy
really

374
00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:54.320
comes into play.

375
00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:58.800
Retaining means deliberately deciding to
self-insure a specific portion of the

376
00:14:58.800 --> 00:14:59.420
potential loss.

377
00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.960
Take Elyph and Adam, for example. When
setting up their home insurance,

378
00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:04.560
their broker gives them a few

379
00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:07.480
choices. Instead of taking the standard,
comfortable,

380
00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:13.060
$500 deductible, they consciously opt for
a much higher, $2,500 deductible to

381
00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:13.580
secure a

382
00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:15.180
substantial discount on their monthly

383
00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:17.960
premium. Why does this matter? Because
they're making a

384
00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:22.960
calculated bet on themselves. They're
actively retaining or self-insuring the
first

385
00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:23.540
$2,500

386
00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:27.920
of any damage. They're totally willing to
absorb that smaller, localized financial

387
00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:28.340
pain

388
00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:30.680
in order to save cash month over month in
the long

389
00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:34.180
run. And finally, we've arrived at section
5,

390
00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:35.880
transferring risk through

391
00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.280
insurance. This is the climax of risk
management,

392
00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:40.040
transferring.

393
00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:44.920
When individuals or businesses look at a
potential disaster and realize they simply

394
00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:49.860
cannot withstand the massive financial
shockwave of a total loss, like, say, an

395
00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:50.420
entire house

396
00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.800
burning down to the foundation, or getting
hit with a million-dollar liability

397
00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:54.360
lawsuit,

398
00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:59.020
they use this fourth method. They transfer
that overwhelming risk to an insurance

399
00:15:59.020 --> 00:15:59.460
company.

400
00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:02.040
And they do this through a beautifully
disproportionate

401
00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.840
mechanism. In exchange for paying a
relatively

402
00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:09.320
small, manageable, predictable premium,
they completely shift the massive,

403
00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:09.920
unpredictable

404
00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:14.760
financial burden of a potential
catastrophe over to a massive institution
that is

405
00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:15.120
financially

406
00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:19.300
designed to absorb it. But there is a very
strict rule governing this

407
00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.020
transfer. And it is arguably

408
00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:25.660
the single most important concept in the
entire textbook, the principle of

409
00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:26.160
indemnity.

410
00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.980
The entire purpose of transferring your
risk is to be

411
00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.260
indemnified. It is a strict contractual

412
00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:35.440
obligation to put you back in the exact
same financial position you were in right

413
00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:35.880
before the

414
00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:37.620
loss occurred. No more, no

415
00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:41.580
less. If you total a 5-year-old car, the
insurance company does not buy you a

416
00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:45.780
brand new car off the showroom floor. They
provide the exact financial value of a 5

417
00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:46.320
-year-old car.

418
00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.820
Insurance is a shield designed for

419
00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:51.100
protection. It is not a lottery ticket
designed for

420
00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:51.400
profit.

421
00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:54.400
You aren't meant to come out ahead. You
are simply meant to be made whole

422
00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:54.660
again.

423
00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:58.320
So as we wrap up this explainer, I really
want to leave you with a provocative

424
00:16:58.320 --> 00:16:58.920
thought.

425
00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:02.300
We learned right at the beginning that
your greatest asset isn't your house,

426
00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:06.480
your business, or your car. It's you, your
health, your ability to earn an income,

427
00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:10.360
your life. Looking closely at the four
practical tools we unpacked today,

428
00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:11.660
eliminating, controlling,

429
00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.680
retaining, and transferring, how are you
currently categorizing and managing the

430
00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:16.560
pure risks in your

431
00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:20.780
own life? I sincerely hope this structured
dive into the foundations of risk has

432
00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:21.240
sparked your

433
00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:25.360
curiosity to deeply evaluate your own
financial safety nets and perhaps look
just a

434
00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:25.780
little closer

435
00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.420
at how you protect the things that matter

436
00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:28.800
most. Thanks for joining me.
